Cardiff entered the [[2011–12 Football League Cup]] in the first round where they faced [[Oxford United F.C.|Oxford United]]. The match played at the [[Kassam Stadium]] saw Cardiff take the lead in the 10th minute when [[Craig Conway (footballer)|Craig Conway]] scored. Oxford equalised 20 minutes later courtesy of a [[Simon Clist]] goal. Neither side was able to score a winning goal during the remainder of the match, which went into [[Overtime (sports)|extra-time]] at 1–1. Two goals from [[Peter Whittingham]] and [[Nathaniel Jarvis]] secured a 3–1 victory for Cardiff.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/14383989 |title=Oxford 1–3 Cardiff |work=BBC Sport |publisher=British Broadcasting Corporation |date=10 August 2011 |access-date=15 April 2012 }}</ref> Their opponents in the second round were [[Huddersfield Town F.C.|Huddersfield Town]]. Two goals in the first 16 minutes courtesy of [[Gabor Gyepes]] and [[Jon Parkin]] gave Cardiff an early 2–0 lead, which they held until the 55th minute when Huddersfield reduced the deficit to 2–1 courtesy of a [[Jordan Rhodes]] goal. [[Danny Ward (footballer, born 1990)|Daniel Ward]] equalised for Huddersfield in the 69th minute before Rhodes scored his second goal of the match to give Huddersfield a 3–2 lead in the 87th minute. Cardiff equalised two minutes into stoppage time when [[Don Cowie (footballer)|Don Cowie]] headed the ball in from close range. With the score at 3–3 after 90 minutes the match went to extra-time.  A goal each from Conway and Cowie gave Cardiff a 5–3 victory and progression to the third round.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/14539104 |title=Cardiff 5–3 Huddersfield |work=BBC Sport |publisher=British Broadcasting Corporation |date=23 August 2011 |access-date=15 April 2012 }}</ref>

[[Leicester City F.C.|Leicester City]] were the opponents in the third round. Cardiff playing at their home ground the [[Cardiff City Stadium]] took the lead in the 32nd minute when Cowie scored. Leicester equalised seven minutes later when [[Steve Howard]] scored from close range. Leicester then took the lead in the 65th minute courtesy of a [[Lloyd Dyer]] goal. Cardiff equalised when [[Rudy Gestede]] with nine minutes of the match remaining. The score remained 2–2 until full-time and throughout extra-time, which mean the match would be decided by a [[Penalty shoot-out (association football)|penalty shoot-out]]. Cardiff won the shoot-out 7–6 to progress to the fourth round.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/14910203 |title=Cardiff 2–2 Leicester (7–6 pens) |work=BBC Sport |publisher=British Broadcasting Corporation |date=21 September 2011 |access-date=15 April 2012 }}</ref>

Cardiff were drawn at home against [[Burnley F.C.|Burnley]] in the fourth round. They won the match 1–0 courtesy of a [[Joe Mason (footballer, born 1991)|Joe Mason]] goal in the first half. The result was the first time in the competition Cardiff's match did not go into extra-time and it was the first they had reached the quarter-finals of the League Cup since 1965.<ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/15346240 |title=Cardiff 1–0 Burnley |work=BBC Sport |publisher=British Broadcasting Corporation |date=25 October 2011 |access-date=15 April 2012 }}</ref> Their opponents in the quarter-finals were [[Blackburn Rovers F.C.|Blackburn Rovers]]. Two goals from [[Kenny Miller]] and [[Anthony Gerrard]] secured a 2–0 victory for Cardiff and their progression to the semi-finals.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/15831896 |title=Cardiff 2–0 Blackburn |work=BBC Sport |publisher=British Broadcasting Corporation |date=29 November 2011 |access-date=15 April 2012 }}</ref>

[[Crystal Palace F.C.|Crystal Palace]] were Cardiff's opponents in the semi-final which was played over two-legs. The first leg was played at Palace's home ground [[Selhurst Park]]. Cardiff started the match the better of the two sides but were unable to convert their chances. Palace took the lead in the 43rd minute when [[Anthony Gardner (footballer)|Anthony Gardner]] headed in [[Darren Ambrose]]'s cross. Neither side scored again and the match ended 1–0.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/16388644 |title=Crystal Palace 1–0 Cardiff |work=BBC Sport |publisher=British Broadcasting Corporation |date=10 January 2012 |access-date=15 April 2012 |first=Alastair |last=Magowan }}</ref> The second leg at the Cardiff City Stadium started well for Cardiff when they went ahead in the seventh minute after an own-goal from Palace defender Gardner, which levelled the aggregate score at 1–1. Palace were reduced to ten men in the second half when [[Paddy McCarthy]] was sent-off. Despite their numerical advantage Cardiff were unable to a second goal during the 90 minutes and extra-time and the tie went to a penalty shoot-out. Cardiff goalkeeper [[Tom Heaton]] saved two penalties as Cardiff won the shoot-out 3–1 to progress to their first League Cup final.<ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/16589189 |title=Cardiff 1–0 Crystal Palace (agg 1–1, pen 3–1) |work=BBC Sport |publisher=British Broadcasting Corporation |date=24 January 2012 |access-date=15 April 2012 }}</ref>

