==Post-match==
[[File:Liverpool players looking for family.jpg|thumb|right|The Liverpool players celebrating their victory after the trophy presentation]]
Liverpool's victory meant they won the trophy for the eighth time, extending their record number of victories in the competition. Midfielder [[Stewart Downing]] was awarded the [[Alan Hardaker Trophy]] as the man of the match.<ref name="Man of match" /> Liverpool manager [[Kenny Dalglish]] was delighted to have won the League Cup and was adamant that the club would continue to win trophies: "Although we have won something today, that is not us finished, we don't want to stop here. We want to keep going. We've won it and we are going to really enjoy it. I know how much the players have enjoyed it and it gives you a wee flavour to come back and do it again." The victory meant that Dalglish became the seventh manager to win all three major domestic trophies in English football.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/17174308 |title=Kenny Dalglish believes Carling Cup win can inspire Liverpool |work=BBC Sport |publisher=British Broadcasting Corporation |date=26 February 2012 |access-date=20 May 2012 |first=Chris |last=Bevan }}</ref> Goalscorer [[Dirk Kuyt]] was equally delighted to have won the competition: "We wanted this so desperately, this is why I came to Anfield. To get my first medal is great." Meanwhile, captain [[Steven Gerrard]] had mixed emotions, due to his cousin [[Anthony Gerrard]] missing the deciding penalty: "One of Anthony or I was going to be sad, one was going to be celebrating. It happens. I've got mixed emotions at the moment because I feel for Anthony and Cardiff."<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/17174010 |title=Liverpool 'desperate' for Carling Cup win – Kuyt |work=BBC Sport |publisher=British Broadcasting Corporation |date=26 February 2012 |access-date=20 May 2012 }}</ref>

Cardiff manager [[Malky Mackay]] was proud of his players despite their defeat: "We wanted to come here and win today but the players have done the club proud, I think today we were playing against a top team and I think we have got a lot to be proud of."<ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/17174391 |title=Malky Mackay proud of Cardiff despite Carling Cup final loss |work=BBC Sport |publisher=British Broadcasting Corporation |date=26 February 2012 |access-date=21 May 2012 }}</ref> [[Anthony Gerrard]], who missed the decisive penalty took to Twitter to apologise to Cardiff fans stating that the miss would "...haunt me for the rest of my days!" Despite Gerrard's apology, Cardiff goalkeeper [[Tom Heaton]] was adamant that none of the players should take the blame for the defeat: "There is definitely no blame attached to any of the lads who missed. They all had the strength of character to stand up and take a penalty in front of 90,000 people and the world watching in a pressure situation. They should be credited and congratulated for taking them. Sadly, it wasn’t our day.”<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.telegraph.co.uk/sport/football/competitions/league-cup/9108476/Liverpool-v-Cardiff-Anthony-Gerrard-says-Carling-Cup-penalty-miss-will-haunt-me-forever.html |title=Anthony Gerrard says Carling Cup penalty miss 'will haunt me forever' |work=The Daily Telegraph |date=27 February 2012 |access-date=21 May 2012 }}</ref>

Liverpool's first match after the final was against [[Arsenal F.C.|Arsenal]], a match the club needed to win to stay in touch with the top four. Two goals from [[Robin van Persie]], after Liverpool had scored in the first half meant that Liverpool lost 2–1 and were 10 points behind the team in 4th place, which would ensure qualification for the [[UEFA Champions League]] the following season.<ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/17163155 |title=Liverpool 1–2 Arsenal |work=BBC Sport |publisher=British Broadcasting Corporation |date=3 March 2012 |access-date=23 May 2012 |first=Chris |last=Bevan }}</ref> Liverpool's league form continued to falter after the final; they won four of their last thirteen matches and ended the season in eighth place.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.lfchistory.net/SeasonArchive/Games/121 |title=Games for the 2011–2012 season |publisher=LFC History |access-date=23 May 2012 }}</ref> Despite their poor league form, Liverpool reached the [[2012 FA Cup final|final]] of the FA Cup against [[Chelsea F.C.|Chelsea]]. But they were unable to win a second trophy and lost 2–1.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/17878435 |title=Chelsea 2–1 Liverpool |work=BBC Sport |publisher=British Broadcasting Corporation |date=5 May 2012 |access-date=23 May 2012 }}</ref> Cardiff played [[West Ham United F.C.|West Ham United]] in their first match after the final, which they lost 2–0.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/17175784 |title=Cardiff 0–2 West Ham |work=BBC Sport |publisher=British Broadcasting Corporation |date=4 March 2012 |access-date=23 May 2012 |first=Peter |last=Shuttleworth }}</ref> Despite the loss, they only needed to win one more in their remaining thirteen matches to finish in 6th place and qualify for the [[Football League Championship playoffs|Championship playoffs]].<ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/tables |title=The Npower Football League Championship: Table |work=BBC Sport |publisher=British Broadcasting Corporation |date=28 April 2012 |access-date=23 May 2012 |archive-date=7 May 2010 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20100507112154/http://news.bbc.co.uk/sport1/hi/football/eng_div_1/table/default.stm |url-status=live }}</ref> They faced third placed West Ham United, in the playoffs, which were contested over two-legs, with one at each team's home ground. Cardiff were unable to secure a second Wembley final, as West Ham United won 5–0 on aggregate.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/17890891 |title=West Ham 3–0 Cardiff (5–0 agg) |work=BBC Sport |publisher=British Broadcasting Corporation |date=7 May 2012 |access-date=23 May 2012 }}</ref>

